22.1 Three windows per item
How much did each item move? For each of the 102 items (reverse-keyed items turned, Chapter 2): Δd while the experimental group trains (T0 to T1), its durability (T0 to T2), and while the control group trains (T2 to T3). In the last window the sign is turned, so that a positive value means a gain of the trained group in every window. The ranking index is the mean of the two training windows.

item_effects &lt;- tribble(
  ~pre, ~post, ~window,   ~sign,
  "T0", "T1",  "T0_T1",   1,
  "T0", "T2",  "T0_T2",   1,
  "T2", "T3",  "T2_T3",  -1
) |&gt;
  mutate(table = pmap(list(pre, post), \(a, b) delta_d(df, a, b, item_names))) |&gt;
  unnest(table) |&gt;
  mutate(delta_d = sign * delta_d) |&gt;
  select(window, item = outcome, delta_d, p) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = window, values_from = c(delta_d, p)) |&gt;
  mutate(training_mean = (delta_d_T0_T1 + delta_d_T2_T3) / 2,
         scale = str_remove(item, "[0-9]+$"),
         scale = scales$label[match(scale, scales$scale)]) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(training_mean)) |&gt;
  mutate(rank = row_number(), .before = 1)

15.1 Calibration sample and rules
On which answers does the new structure rest? On the T0 questionnaires of both groups: everyone answered them before the first training, so no training effect can shape the structure. The later waves serve to check whether the structure repeats.

